{
  "id": "u01-l01",
  "unit": "u01-basics",
  "title": "Hello & goodbye",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "**আসসালামু আলাইকুম** (*assalamu alaikum*, 'peace be upon you') is the everyday hello in Bangladesh; the reply is **ওয়ালাইকুম আসসালাম** (*walaikum assalam*). Hindu Bengalis, and most people in Kolkata, say **নমস্কার** (*nomoshkar*) instead.\n\nFor 'yes', **জি** (*ji*) is the polite choice with elders, teachers and strangers; **হ্যাঁ** (*hya~*) is neutral. **না** (*na*) is 'no' and **ধন্যবাদ** (*dhonnobad*) is 'thank you'.\n\nTo say goodbye, **খোদা হাফেজ** (*khoda hafez*, 'may God protect you') is usual in Bangladesh. **আবার দেখা হবে** (*abar dekha hobe*, 'see you again') works for everyone.",
  "vocab": [
    {
      "id": "assalamu-alaikum",
      "bn": "আসসালামু আলাইকুম",
      "tr": "assalamu alaikum",
      "en": "hello (peace be upon you)",
      "notes": "The standard greeting in Bangladesh, especially among Muslims. Often shortened to সালাম (salam)."
    },
    {
      "id": "walaikum-assalam",
      "bn": "ওয়ালাইকুম আসসালাম",
      "tr": "walaikum assalam",
      "en": "hello (in reply)",
      "notes": "Reply to আসসালামু আলাইকুম: 'and peace be upon you too'."
    },
    {
      "id": "nomoshkar",
      "bn": "নমস্কার",
      "tr": "nomoshkar",
      "en": "hello (Hindu greeting)",
      "notes": "Used by Hindus in Bangladesh; the usual formal greeting in West Bengal (Kolkata). Said with palms pressed together."
    },
    {
      "id": "hya",
      "bn": "হ্যাঁ",
      "tr": "hya~",
      "en": "yes",
      "notes": "Nasal vowel, like 'hæ' said through the nose."
    },
    {
      "id": "ji",
      "bn": "জি",
      "tr": "ji",
      "en": "yes (polite)",
      "notes": "Respectful 'yes' used widely in Bangladesh; also spelled জ্বি. Rare in Kolkata."
    },
    {
      "id": "na",
      "bn": "না",
      "tr": "na",
      "en": "no"
    },
    {
      "id": "dhonnobad",
      "bn": "ধন্যবাদ",
      "tr": "dhonnobad",
      "en": "thank you",
      "notes": "Between close family and friends, Bengalis thank each other less than English speakers do; ধন্যবাদ can sound formal there."
    },
    {
      "id": "khoda-hafez",
      "bn": "খোদা হাফেজ",
      "tr": "khoda hafez",
      "en": "goodbye",
      "notes": "Lit. 'may God protect you'. Variant: আল্লাহ হাফেজ (allah hafez). The z sound in hafez comes from Arabic; many speakers say 'hafej'."
    },
    {
      "id": "abar-dekha-hobe",
      "bn": "আবার দেখা হবে",
      "tr": "abar dekha hobe",
      "en": "see you again",
      "notes": "Lit. 'there will be a meeting again'. Neutral, used by everyone in both Bangladesh and West Bengal."
    }
  ],
